{
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "route": {
    "route_id": "cairo-aswan",
    "name": "Cairo-Aswan Railway (Upper Egypt Main Line)",
    "name_arabic": "سكة حديد القاهرة-أسوان",
    "countries": "Egypt",
    "gauge_mm": 1435,
    "year_opened": 1874,
    "era": "historic",
    "color": "#b45309",
    "corridor_confidence": "high",
    "description": "Upper Egypt Nile-valley trunk; luxury 'Sunshine Express'",
    "notes": "ADDED as a 12th route from the Railway Wonders article + Wikipedia station list. Not in the original project 11; confirm you want it in the canonical set.  [established] Built in stages: Cairo–Assiut by 1874 (230 mi); Assiut–Luxor by 1898 (340 mi, Nile crossing at Nag Hammadi); Kena–Aswan by the private Kena-Aswan Railway Company; Aswan–Shellal was an 1884 military line, absorbed 1898.  [gauge_mm] Now standard gauge (1,435 mm). Historically MIXED: Kena–Luxor standard; Luxor–Aswan 3 ft 6 in (1,067 mm); Aswan–Shellal narrow gauge, regauged to standard in 1926 (railwaywonders).  [route] Stops from the Railway Wonders article (Cairo, Assiut, Nag Hammadi, Qena, Luxor, Aswan, Shellal) plus the modern main-line order from Wikipedia’s Egyptian station list (Beni Suef, Minya, Mallawi, Sohag, Esna, Edfu, Kom Ombo).",
    "history": "The Upper Egypt Nile-valley main line, built southward in stages: Cairo–Assiut by 1874, Assiut–Luxor by 1898 (crossing the Nile at Nag Hammadi). The Kena–Aswan section was built by the private Kena-Aswan Railway Company; the Aswan–Shellal section began as an 1884 military line for the first Sudan campaign and was regauged to standard gauge in 1926. By the mid-1930s three trains a day ran each way Cairo–Luxor (~12 hours) and two a day to Aswan/Shellal (480 miles, ~17 hours).",
    "verification_status": "unverified",
    "source_ids": [
      "levantrain_compilation",
      "railwaywonders",
      "wp_egypt_stations"
    ]
  },
